      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\), we need to determine \\( c \\) such that the quadratic equation \\( x^2 + 3x + c = -2 \\) has at least one real solution. \n\nRewriting the equation, we have:\n\\[\nx^2 + 3x + c + 2 = 0\n\\]\nThis equation can be written in standard form as:\n\\[\nx^2 + 3x + (c + 2) = 0\n\\]\n\nFor this quadratic equation to have at least one real solution, the discriminant must be non-negative. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of a quadratic equation \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\n\\]\n\nIn our equation, \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 3 \\), and \\( c = c + 2 \\). Substituting these values into the discriminant formula, we get:\n\\[\n\\Delta = 3^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (c + 2) = 9 - 4(c + 2)\n\\]\nSimplifying further:\n\\[\n\\Delta = 9 - 4c - 8 = 1 - 4c\n\\]\n\nFor the quadratic equation to have at least one real solution, the discriminant must be non-negative:\n\\[\n1 - 4c \\geq 0\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( c \\):\n\\[\n1 \\geq 4c\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{4} \\geq c\n\\]\n\nThus, the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}}\n\\]",
