      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\( -2 \\) is in the range of \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\), we need to determine when \\( -2 \\) can be expressed as \\( f(x) \\) for some \\( x \\). That is, we need:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 3x + c = -2\n\\]\n\nThis equation can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 3x + c + 2 = 0\n\\]\n\nFor \\( x^2 + 3x + c + 2 = 0 \\) to have real solutions, its discriminant must be non-negative. The discriminant \\( \\Delta \\) of a quadratic equation \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\n\\]\n\nIn our case, \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 3 \\), and \\( c = c + 2 \\). Thus, the discriminant is:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 3^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (c + 2)\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expression:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 9 - 4(c + 2)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Delta = 9 - 4c - 8\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Delta = 1 - 4c\n\\]\n\nFor \\( x^2 + 3x + c + 2 = 0 \\) to have real solutions, the discriminant must be non-negative:\n\n\\[\n1 - 4c \\geq 0\n\\]\n\nSolving this inequality:\n\n\\[\n1 \\geq 4c\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{4} \\geq c\n\\]\n\nThe largest value of \\( c \\) satisfying this inequality is:\n\n\\[\nc = \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\n\nThus, the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\( -2 \\) is in the range of \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}}\n\\]",
